  "step_3": "First, comparing the information relevant to the decision reason: The reason requires evaluating skewness—specifically, whether a greater portion of probability is assigned to favorable outcomes (less negative) versus unfavorable ones. In this case, the favorable outcome (0) in Lottery B has only 20% probability, while the unfavorable outcome (-4000) has 80%. Lottery A has no favorable outcome and no variation. The individual did not consider or mention the distribution of probabilities in terms of skewness or the shape of the distribution. Instead, they focused on certainty and risk. Second, comparing the decision rule to the justification: The reason prefers the more left-skewed lottery (greater probability on favorable outcomes). The individual’s justification is based on certainty and risk avoidance, not on the skewness of the distribution. There is no indication that they evaluated or compared the probability mass across outcomes in terms of skewness. The individual’s reasoning aligns with risk aversion and certainty equivalence, not with a formal assessment of skewness. Therefore, the decision reason based on skewness was not used in the individual’s deliberation.",
